.TH tuholiday n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tuholiday
.PP
German public holidays from the Easter computus. Computes Easter Sunday (Gregorian, Meeus/Jones/Butcher algorithm) and derives the movable feasts, then returns the German holidays of a year: the nine statutory in all states, by default with Easter Sunday and Whit Sunday (\fB\-sundays 1\fR). Pure Tcl on top of \fBclock\fR.
.SS API
.CS
::tclutils::tuholiday::easter 2026             ;# -> 2026-04-05
::tclutils::tuholiday::holidays 2026           ;# -> dict {ISO-date -> name}, sorted, 11 days
::tclutils::tuholiday::holidays 2026 -sundays 0 ;# -> the 9 days statutory in all states
::tclutils::tuholiday::isHoliday 2026-01-01    ;# -> "Neujahr"  ("" if none)
.CE
.PP
Commands:
.IP \(bu
\fBeaster year\fR — Easter Sunday of \fByear\fR as an ISO date (\fBYYYY\-MM\-DD\fR).
.IP \(bu
\fBholidays year ?\-region de? ?\-sundays 0|1?\fR — the German holidays as a dict mapping ISO date → name, ordered by date (Neujahr, Karfreitag, Oster\-/Pfingst\-Tage, Christi Himmelfahrt, Tag der Arbeit, Tag der Deutschen Einheit, beide Weihnachtstage).
.IP \(bu
\fBisHoliday iso ?\-region de? ?\-sundays 0|1?\fR — the holiday name for an ISO date, or \fB""\fR if it is not a holiday.
.PP
Scope: eleven days by default: the nine statutory in \fBall\fR German states plus Easter Sunday and Whit Sunday (Sundays anyway; statutory in a few states only). \fB\-sundays 0\fR (since 0.2) leaves the two Sundays out; that is the list of \fBtical::holidays::de\fR without options. State\-specific days (Heilige Drei Könige, Fronleichnam, Allerheiligen, Reformationstag, …) are intentionally not in the base set; \fB\-region\fR currently accepts only \fBde\fR.
.PP
Errors: a non\-integer year → \fB{TCLUTILS TUHOLIDAY YEAR}\fR; an unknown region → \fB{TCLUTILS TUHOLIDAY REGION}\fR; \fB\-sundays\fR not 0 or 1 → \fB{TCLUTILS TUHOLIDAY SUNDAYS}\fR.
